xnu 0236: Mesa's own 2D clear, replayed from the kernel

Nothing Mesa draws reaches memory -- with the 2D blitter and with the generic
3D one -- while the CP's own writes land and the RB is measured pointing at
the surface with the surface's pitch. A pattern written into the surface
survives a whole frame: nothing writes it, no clear included. Two
explanations are left, and they differ in the 2D engine, which needs no
shader, viewport or binning: the draw state never makes the RB write, or the
colour path to DRAM is broken on this GPU.

So the test is Mesa's own clear, byte for byte: the window server's first
frame carries a 51-dword clearing IB, and this replays those exact dwords
with only the destination address and the solid colour changed. Nothing here
re-derives a register value -- the first attempt did, wrote SP_2D_DST_FORMAT
at 0xacc0 (the a6xx offset; the a730 has it at 0xa9bf), and parked the CP for
1.6 s until every later submission failed with `submit failed: -35`.

gpu2dfill=1 fills a 0x20000-byte buffer of the driver's own and then, when a
surface has been adopted, the memory the display reads: the first says
whether the engine writes, the second whether the surface is memory it can
write. The buffer is read back even when the poll fails, because the answer
is what the engine wrote, not whether the fence answered.
